Backport from mainline Linux (CIX upstream work for the "CPU DVFS" row of the
wiki; Sky1 CPU frequency scaling goes through the SCMI performance protocol to
the SCP firmware): 189df98777a "firmware: arm_scmi: Check beforehand if the
perf domain set operations are allowed" (author: xinglong.yang@cixtech.com).

The firmware reports in PERF_DOMAIN_ATTRIBUTES whether Linux may set the level
and/or the limits of each performance domain. Linux ignored that and sent
PERF_LEVEL_SET / PERF_LIMITS_SET anyway, which the SCP then rejected. Now such
requests fail with -EOPNOTSUPP without a round trip to the SCP, which takes load
off the SCMI channel (the same SCP whose stalls under load patch 0003 addresses).

diff --git a/drivers/firmware/arm_scmi/perf.c b/drivers/firmware/arm_scmi/perf.c
index 096867e81..ae8ec4979 100644
--- a/drivers/firmware/arm_scmi/perf.c
+++ b/drivers/firmware/arm_scmi/perf.c
@@ -521,6 +521,9 @@ static int scmi_perf_limits_set(const struct scmi_protocol_handle *ph,
 	if (IS_ERR(dom))
 		return PTR_ERR(dom);
 
+	if (!dom->set_limits)
+		return -EOPNOTSUPP;
+
 	if (PROTOCOL_REV_MAJOR(pi->version) >= 0x3 && !max_perf && !min_perf)
 		return -EINVAL;
 
@@ -671,6 +674,9 @@ static int scmi_perf_level_set(const struct scmi_protocol_handle *ph,
 	if (IS_ERR(dom))
 		return PTR_ERR(dom);
 
+	if (!dom->info.set_perf)
+		return -EOPNOTSUPP;
+
 	if (dom->level_indexing_mode) {
 		struct scmi_opp *opp;
 
